# Copy to .env and edit.
#
# HOW THIS FILE IS READ (important):
#  - DOCKER: compose uses this file for ${VAR} substitution (ports/volumes) and injects into the container
#    ONLY the vars listed under `environment:` in docker-compose.yml. A server tuning var that ISN'T in that
#    list (most of the "advanced tuning" block below) has NO effect under Docker until you add it there.
#  - NATIVE (CC_RUNTIME=native): the service sources this whole file, so every var here takes effect.
#  - The coach/responder host-side sidecars also source this file (EnvironmentFile=.env).

# Timezone for the cost "today"/daily rollups (so they roll over at YOUR local midnight, not UTC).
# Use a tz database name, e.g. America/Chicago, Europe/London. Defaults to UTC if unset.
TZ=UTC

# Label shown on the dashboard for this machine (the container can't see the real host name).
CC_HOST=claude-host

# Which Claude account this host uses (drives the per-account rate-limit gauges + card tags). Normally
# auto-detected: the statusline hook reads ~/.claude.json host-side, and a native watcher falls back to
# reading it directly. Set this ONLY to override — e.g. on the dashboard CONTAINER's local-scan host, where
# ~/.claude.json isn't mounted in (by design — keeps OAuth tokens out of the container) and you'd otherwise
# want idle sessions tagged too. Leave blank to auto-detect.
# CC_ACCOUNT=you@example.com

# Port to expose the dashboard on.
CC_PORT=8099

# Comma-separated model-id substrings to treat as a 1M-context window
# (transcripts omit the [1m] marker). Leave blank if you don't use 1M models.
CC_WINDOW_1M_MODELS=claude-opus-4-8

# Override only if your transcripts live somewhere other than ~/.claude/projects
# CC_PROJECTS_DIR=/home/youruser/.claude/projects

# --- Basic security (optional; Local-First default is open on a trusted LAN) ---
# Set a PIN to require unlock for the dashboard UI (first visit prompts, then a long-lived cookie).
# IMPORTANT: this PIN also gates /reply (which injects keystrokes into your tmux pane = code execution),
# and the dashboard listens on the LAN. Use a LONG, non-numeric secret — NOT a 4-digit PIN. Failed logins
# are rate-limited per-IP (CC_LOGIN_MAX_FAILS / _WINDOW / _BLOCK), but a short PIN is still a bad idea.
# Generate one, e.g.  openssl rand -hex 16
# CC_ACCESS_PIN=change-me-to-a-long-random-passphrase
# Set a shared token to require remote watchers to send  X-CC-Token: <token>  on /ingest.
# This can be ANY text — just make it long and random (recommend >24 characters). You never type it
# or need to remember it: it's used purely programmatically (the watcher and responder read it from
# this file to authenticate). Generate one however you like, e.g.  openssl rand -hex 24
# CC_INGEST_TOKEN=change-me-to-a-long-random-string
# Rotate this to INVALIDATE all existing login cookies (e.g. a device was lost / a cookie leaked) WITHOUT
# changing the PIN — any new value logs every device out. Leave unset for the default.
# CC_AUTH_SALT=

# --- Answer-from-phone (E5) ---
# Tap a waiting session on your phone and type a reply -> injected into its tmux pane via the
# cc-responder service (./deploy-responder.sh). This is RCE into your shell, so it is REFUSED
# unless CC_ACCESS_PIN is set above. It ALSO REQUIRES CC_INGEST_TOKEN (the reply/coach control-plane
# refuses when the token is unset, so a LAN host can't drain or forge replies).
# The responder reads CC_HOST + CC_INGEST_TOKEN from THIS file. Only sessions running inside tmux
# (with the state hook recording their pane) are answerable.

# --- Local transcript search (opt-in feature) ---
# The 🔍 Search scene indexes your transcripts into a SEPARATE on-disk DB and serves them on a second,
# loopback-ONLY port (never exposed on the LAN — the "content firewall"). Default 8100. Change this ONLY
# if 8100 is already in use on this machine.
# CC_CONTENT_PORT=8100
#
# Optional semantic (meaning-based) search needs a LOCAL Ollama running an embedding model — this is still
# zero third-party egress (it's your own local model). Leave both unset for keyword-only search.
# CC_EMBED_MODEL=nomic-embed-text
# CC_OLLAMA_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434

# --- Advanced: relocate data on the host (all optional; sensible defaults shown) ---
# CC_DB_DIR=~/.cache/cc-observability            # usage/cost history DB (read-write)
# CC_STATE_DIR_HOST=~/.cache/cc-dashboard        # statusline/state snapshots (read-only mount)
# CC_EXPORT_DIR_HOST=~/cc-observability-exports  # encrypted export bundles land here

# --- Self-update check (opt-in) ---
# Opt-in manifest URL for the update checker. Leave unset (default) for no check.
# When set, the server polls this URL every 6 h (CC_UPDATE_INTERVAL seconds), reads
# {"version":"x.y.z"} from the response, and surfaces a banner if a newer release exists.
# Outbound GET of a tiny JSON only — zero user data is ever sent; never auto-applies.
# CC_UPDATE_URL=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/johndelay/jaid-observability-platform/main/manifest.json

# --- Native runtime (no Docker) ---
# Set when running natively (the jaid-setup skill / install-user-services.sh do this). Surfaces in /health
# and binds the content-search port straight to 127.0.0.1 (no Docker dual-port). Leave unset under Docker.
# CC_RUNTIME=native

# --- In-app coach (opt-in; read by the host-side cc-coach-worker, NOT the container) ---
# The 🧠 Coach scene's narrative is generated by a native worker (./deploy-coach-worker.sh) using YOUR own
# Claude or a local Ollama — the container can't run either. Engine resolves automatically:
#   auto  -> `claude` CLI if on PATH (uses your tokens, kept lean) -> else Ollama if CC_OLLAMA_URL set -> off
# COACH_ENGINE=auto                      # auto | claude | ollama | off
# CC_CLAUDE_BIN=claude                   # path to your Claude CLI (if not on PATH)
# CC_COACH_CLAUDE_MODEL=                 # optional: pin the model `claude -p` uses (blank = your default)
# CC_OLLAMA_MODEL=hermes3:8b-16k         # model for the Ollama coach engine (must be pulled locally)
# (Semantic-search Ollama settings are CC_EMBED_MODEL / CC_OLLAMA_URL above — separate from the coach.)

# --- Behavior toggles ---
# "Needs you" = Claude is genuinely BLOCKED on you (a permission prompt or an AskUserQuestion), not just that
# a turn finished. Set to 0 to revert to the old behavior where any completed turn surfaces in the hero.
# (Pre-wired into docker-compose.yml's environment list, so it works under Docker too.)
# CC_STRICT_NEEDS_ME=1

# --- Advanced tuning (rarely needed; defaults shown). NATIVE reads these from here; for DOCKER add the
#     specific one you change to docker-compose.yml's `environment:` list, or it won't reach the container. ---
# CC_COMPACT_THRESHOLD=0.95     # fraction of the window where auto-compaction fires
# CC_WINDOW_DEFAULT=200000      # assumed window when the model isn't a known 1M model
# CC_POLL_INTERVAL=10           # seconds between transcript scans (context creeps slowly — keep it light)
# CC_ACTIVE_HOURS=6             # only report sessions touched within this many hours
# CC_STALE_SECS=120             # no update within this → a card dims
# CC_EXPIRE_SECS=21600          # drop a session from view after this many idle seconds (6 h)
# CC_WAITING_RECENT_SECS=1800   # a "waiting" turn older than this → demoted from the hero to idle (30 min)
# CC_SSE_INTERVAL=5             # how often the live stream checks for changes (sends only on change)
# CC_SSE_HEARTBEAT=30           # keep-alive ping when nothing changed
# CC_SUBAGENT_RUNNING_SECS=30   # a subagent file modified within this = "running"
# CC_SUBAGENT_WINDOW=900        # show subagents active within this many seconds
# CC_HOST_FRESH_SECS=45         # Fleet scene: heard from a host within this = 🟢 green
# CC_HOST_STALE_SECS=180        # Fleet scene: silent past this = 🔴 red (watcher down)
# CC_UPDATE_INTERVAL=21600      # how often to poll CC_UPDATE_URL (6 h)
# (More internal knobs exist — poll/timeout/cap sizes. See the os.environ.get() calls in the source.)
